WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:09.450
Following another choice tweet and a new
tell-all book, a group of psychiatrists

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.950
are publicly warning – now more than ever –
that the president is dangerous, and

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.940
urging that he be removed from access to
weapons. These psychiatrists are

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.990
expressing a view that millions of
Americans share – and millions reject – and

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.609
claiming that their expertise as mental
health professionals adds authority. But

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:31.289
does it? As a psychiatrist, I say no. They
have no greater insight into Trump's

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:35.850
behavior, and thus his fitness for office.
When they claim to, they politicize our

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.450
profession. His grandiosity and
impulsivity were evident during the

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.770
campaign and earlier. They're his baseline
features! Had his personality undergone

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:48.660
rapid deterioration, affected his ability
to govern, then the Cabinet and Congress

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.260
would likely ask psychiatrists for a
diagnosis, treatment options, and a

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.489
prognosis in the course of invoking the
25th Amendment to deprive him of his

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:01.289
powers. But ultimately, this is a
political process, not a medical one. Whatever

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.250
one thinks of Mr. Trump, his actions
and words are on display for all to see.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.260
The public doesn't need experts to
interpret them.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.760
Should psychiatrists comment publicly on the mental health of the president? Let us know in your

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.729
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:22.619
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:25.310
from AEI.

